<<problem 1: Problem 2: List Reversal 

<<blueprint
requires: 
lst is a list of any type 'a

ensures:
- if empty list, return empty list 
- if containing some element, return a new list containing elements of lst in reverse order
- length of returned list = length of lst
blueprint>>



<<operational steps
- Base Case [Empty list] - If empty, return empty
- Base Case [Single Element] - If 1 element, return the same list
- Recursive Case 
  - First element = head and the rest of the list = tail
  - Append the head to the end of the reversed tail accumulatively 
  - Return the results 

- Using tail recursion in this case (because recursive call is the last operation)
- Example used for me to understand:
    - reverse_helper [1; 2; 3] []
    - reverse_helper [2; 3] [1] (prepend 1 to [])
    - reverse_helper [3] [2; 1] (prepend 2 to )​
    - reverse_helper [] [3; 2; 1] (prepend 3 to [2; 1])
    - Return [3; 2; 1] (base case reached)
operational steps>>



<<ocaml code
let reverse (lst : 'a list) : 'a list =
  let rec reverse_helper (lst : 'a list) (acc : 'a list) : 'a list =
    match lst with
    | [] -> acc                           
    | head :: tail -> reverse_helper tail (head :: acc)
  in
  reverse_helper lst []
ocaml code>>



<<proof
INDUCTION
  Base Case 1 - An empty list reversed is still empty, hence proved
  Base Case 2 - A single element list reversed is still the same list, hence proved
  
  Inductive hypothesis 
    - Assume for any list length k, the function reverses the list 
  
  Inductive Step
    - For a list length k + 1, it would be of the structure of head :: tail, wiith the tail being k, so that it would be head + tail, or k + 1
    - When doing reverse tail, the tail is reversed
    - The head then can be appended to the reversed tail, such that reverse tail :: head
    - Therefore, the first element of the original list is now at the end, thus reversing the whole list

TERMINATION
  - The function calls itself recursively with the tail of the list, which is smaller than the original list.
  - Eventually the recursion reaches the base case of an empty list and stops the recurison
  - This terminates the reverse function for all finite lists 
proof>>




